  "name": "02 — Foundation: Commercial terms",
  "text": "Published at relevestaffing.com/terms, effective 1 September 2026,\nand enforced inside the platform. No attorney has reviewed them.\n\nDeposit\n\n$500, taken once a search begins, credited against the first invoice. Non-refundable, with one exception (Sage, 19 October 2026): refunded in full if Relève does not present any candidate for the role.\n\nPlans and fees\n\nThree plans (18 October 2026), replacing the two price bands. Essential, $2,500 a month: unlimited hours from a dedicated, non-specialized VA, six-month commitment. Specialist, $4,500 a month: unlimited hours from a dedicated, specialized expert, six-month commitment. Starter (renamed from Part-Time, 19 October 2026): $950 a month, or $2,500 if paid in full for each three-month term (three-month commitment), the VA's hours capped at 15 a week. The Offer desk has two Part-Time choices: monthly ($950 retainers) and paid in full (one $2,500 invoice per term). eLIVate Club members: $500 off each month for the first three months on Essential and Specialist ($2,000 and $4,000), shown on the Relève × eLIVate page. Tick “eLIVate Club member” on the Offer desk and part42-elivate.sql takes it off the invoices automatically, prorated by service day. Starter direct-hire fee: $11,400, one year at $950 a month (Sage, 19 October 2026), on the FAQ and in Terms Section 6. The plan is confirmed in writing before the placement starts and fixed for the initial term. See the Decisions log for what is still open.\n\nBilling\n\nInvoices issued the first Monday of each month, due on receipt.\n\nMinimum term\n\nSix months on Essential and Specialist, three months on Part-Time, from the start date, payable in full even if ended early.\n\nAfter that\n\nRenews in three-month terms, or the client switches plans at the end of any term (Sage, 19 October 2026). Either side can choose not to renew with 30 days' written notice before the term ends; the placement then runs and bills to the last day of that term. Built into the app 19 October 2026 (supabase/part41-renewals.sql, applied to production): placement_term_end() finds the term a date falls in, notice_end_date() now ends at the first term ending at least 30 days after notice, and Part-Time is invoiced upfront again ($2,500) at the start of each three-month renewal. Monthly plans keep monthly invoices through the renewal terms. TERMS_VERSION is 2026-10-19, so clients re-accept the updated terms.\n\nWhat it covers\n\nSourcing, assessment, matching, interview coordination and ongoing management.\nThe fee is paid to Relève, not the talent — Relève pays them directly.\n\nLate payment\n\nUnpaid for 14 days and the placement may be suspended.\n\nNon-circumvention\n\n12 months from introduction. Engaging that person outside Relève —\ndirectly, via an affiliate, or any third party — owes a fee equal to\ntwelve months of the applicable rate.\n\nGoverning law\n\nState of Utah, United States. Changed from California on 15 September\n2026: the business is based in Utah. Venue moved with it, in the terms and in\nboth markdown sources.\n\nRegistered address\n\nNot published, deliberately. The postal address was removed from\n/terms and /privacy on 15 September 2026 because it was residential and\npublicly exposed. The contact block is now the company name, the country and\nhello@relevestaffing.com. Do not put a street address back until a\ncommercial one exists — a virtual mailbox or a registered-agent business\naddress. Never a home address, and never a PO box, which some payment\nprocessors reject. 29 October 2026: Relève now has a business address, 11116 S Conestoga Dr, Spanish Fork, UT 84660, used only where an address is legally required (the campaign email footer); still not published on the site, Terms or Privacy, by Nona's choice.\n\nHave a Utah attorney read Section 5 and Section 6 before you take\nthe first deposit. Non-circumvention is still the clause most likely to be\ntested, and its duration and scope have to read as reasonable to hold. The move to\nUtah helps here: California voids most restraints on trade, which made this clause\nthe weakest thing in the terms, and Utah enforces reasonable restrictive covenants.\nIt went from probably unenforceable to probably enforceable."
